    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so for the live load, if i'm planning on having a hot tub on one section of the deck, how much extra weight do i need to be factoring in for that compared to just people standing around?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, a hot tub! That is a *massive* addition to your deck, and honestly, it's the kind of load that would make a standard goblin's tiny, one-legged stool wobble right off the edge of the world. You're absolutely right to worry about this; a hot tub isn't just a few people sitting there—it's a heavy, water-filled beast that stays put, unlike the fleeting weight of a human guest.\n\nFor your sizing calculations, you need to factor in the **dead load** of the tub itself (the fiberglass or acrylic shell, the plumbing, the pump, and the motor) plus the **live load** of the water and the people inside. While standard building codes often assume a live load of 40 psf (pounds per square foot) for decks, a hot tub can easily push that to 100 psf or more depending on how full it gets and how many people are lounging in it.\n\nThink of it like this: A group of people standing around is like a few goblins trying to climb a tree—they might jump around, but they aren't heavy enough to snap the branches. But a hot tub is like a giant, wet,",
